//
// The library review reported 334 published `pub fn` "carrying no doc comment".  Almost all
// of them were documented; the READER could not see them.
//
// `doc_block_above` accepted only `///`, and the loft sources overwhelmingly use plain `//` —
// measured across the distribution, 936 `pub fn` are documented that way against 54 with
// `///`.  It also stopped at a blank line, which these sources routinely leave between the
// comment and the declaration.  Between them, a public surface that is documented read as a
// public surface that is not, and the number pointed a reviewer at 334 functions that mostly
// did not need writing.
//
// The failure mode worth remembering: a measurement that disagrees with the project's own
// baseline is a claim about the MEASUREMENT first.  API_SURFACE.md records 44 of the
// stdlib's user-facing functions as having no doc comment, and the tool said nearly all of
// them did.  One of the two was wrong, and it was not the sources.
//
// This file is the loft-visible half — `loft def` is what a user runs.  The Rust-side cells
// live in tests/lsp_doc_block.rs, which can reach the shapes a .loft file cannot express.
//
// @falsified-at: none — this pins a READER, not a behaviour of the language, so no loft
//   program can fail on a build where the reader is wrong; the assertions below would pass
//   whatever `loft def` answered.  Its Rust twin carries the falsification.

fn main() {
  // Documented with `//` — the spelling 936 of the distribution's public functions use.
  assert(abs(-3) == 3, "the function itself still works");
  // Documented with `//` and separated from its declaration by a blank line.
  assert(exp(0.0f) == 1.0f, "…and so does the one with a blank line above it");
  print("152 doc-reader ok\n");
}
